meta {
  name: 6 Set working week (per weekday)
  type: http
  seq: 6
}
put {
  url: {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/providers/{{providerEmail}}/work-week
  body: json
}
body:json {
  {
    "days": [
      { "day": "Monday",    "startHour": 9,  "endHour": 17, "isClosed": false },
      { "day": "Tuesday",   "startHour": 9,  "endHour": 17, "isClosed": false },
      { "day": "Wednesday", "startHour": 9,  "endHour": 17, "isClosed": false },
      { "day": "Thursday",  "startHour": 9,  "endHour": 17, "isClosed": false },
      { "day": "Friday",    "startHour": 9,  "endHour": 13, "isClosed": false },
      { "day": "Saturday",  "startHour": 10, "endHour": 14, "isClosed": false },
      { "day": "Sunday",    "startHour": 9,  "endHour": 17, "isClosed": true  }
    ]
  }
}
docs {
  Per-weekday hours. A **dedicated route** for the same reason `5 Set work hours` is one: `PUT /{email}`
  replaces the whole document, so saving a calendar through it would carry every defect of a
  whole-document write. This is a targeted `$set` on `work_week`.

  **Both routes coexist on purpose.** The single pair from #5 remains the server's fallback for any weekday
  this request does not mention, which is what keeps every provider stored before per-weekday hours existed
  bookable on exactly the hours they had. Sending a partial week is therefore a coherent request:

      { "days": [ { "day": "Saturday", "startHour": 10, "endHour": 14, "isClosed": false } ] }

  …sets Saturday and leaves the rest of the week alone.

  ⚠️ `endHour` is **exclusive**: 17 means the last session finishes at 17:00. Whole hours only — the slot
  grid steps by the hour, so a half-past start has nowhere to land.

  ⚠️ **A day is closed by the flag, not by omitting its hours.** The two are different statements: closed
  means "I do not work Sundays", while a day absent from the list keeps whatever it had. Hours sent
  alongside `isClosed` are kept, so re-opening a day does not mean re-entering them.

  `400` when a day that is not closed does not open before it closes — **refused, never clamped** — and the
  message names the day. Also `400` on an unrecognised weekday name, or the same weekday listed twice
  (picking one silently would store hours nobody chose). `403` unless `{email}` is your own claim.

  Verify with `3-Calendar/1 Availability`: the Friday slots should stop at 13:00 and Sunday should offer
  none at all.
}
